	title: 'Import old Hal sessions',
	spec: "scripts/import-old <old-state> <new-home> (src/import-old.ts) converts the old Hal's sessions (<old-state>/sessions/<id>/) into a Hal home's sessions/, run by hand; nothing on the host path imports or calls it. Each old session becomes one Hal session with the same id, whose history holds exactly what the old Hal would show and send for it: the records of its current log (session.ason currentLog, default history.asonl; earlier logs were superseded by /rebase and a reset and are not imported), preceded, when that log starts with forked_from { parent, ts }, by the parent's full imported history filtered to records older than ts, recursively (the old loadAllHistory). Record contents in blobs/<blobId>.ason (thinking, tool calls and results, errors; for fork-prefix records, the parent's blobs) are written inline. Old records map to Hal records (common/replay.ts): user parts to user blocks (images to Hal image blobs, the bytes copied), assistant text, thinking and tool_call to one assistant record per block, tool_result to user tool_result blocks, turn_end to turn_end (aborted and stopped as paused, failed as error), reset and compact to reset (the old Hal wrote no compact records), cwd and model to change; info, log, warning, error, question and answer, synthetic assistant text and records the old Hal showed only in its UI (visibility 'ui') to output (error: true for errors), so no imported question is live; an inbox prompt (source) keeps its sender as from and label; a prompt the old Hal took while tool calls ran (steering) is placed after their results, so replay never cuts the calls off; a fork starts its own records with an output naming its parent; every history ends idle: one whose last turn has no end gets a paused turn_end, or the first host would continue it and run the model unasked; usage, input_history, pending_tools, forked_to, rebased_from and rebased_to carry nothing the transcript or the model needs and are dropped. Every imported history replays (common/replay.ts) to provider messages where every tool call has its result, and records are numbered n as history.append would. session.ason gets id, cwd (workingDir), model, name and createdAt. The sessions listed in <old-state>/ipc/state.ason become the new home's open tabs (state/tabs.ason), in that order; all others are closed. The script refuses a <new-home> that already has sessions, never writes under <old-state>, and prints a count of sessions, records and bytes, never session content. Tests build small old-format fixtures by hand (no real session data in the repo): a fork of a fork, a rebased session, a blob-backed tool result, an image, an open question.",
